  "text_md": "### 16 ALEXIA YAVICOLI AND HAN YU\n\n0.5\n\n1.0\n\n1.0\n\n1.0 1.5 2.0 2.5\n\n0.5\n\n0.5\n\n-0.5\n\n-0.5 0.5 1.0 1.5 2.0 2.5\n\n0.5 1.0 1.5 2.0 2.5\n\n-0.5\n\n-0.5\n\n-1.0\n\n-1.0\n\n-1.0\n\n(a) Missing 0\n\n(b) Missing 1\n\n## (c) Missing 2\n\n1.0\n\n1.0\n\n0.5\n\n0.5\n\n-0.5 0.5 1.0 1.5 2.0 2.5\n\n-0.5 0.5 1.0 1.5 2.0 2.5\n\n-0.5\n\n-0.5\n\n-1.0\n\n-1.0\n\n(d) Missing 1 + i\n\n(e) Missing 2 + i\n\nFigure 3. Base b = 1+2i with all possible one missing digit.\n\nof Hausdorff the metric. With one missing digit, Cb,D is 1/N1/2(b)close to Cb,D\n\n. Notice that Cb,D is self-similar whose contraction ratio is 1/N1/2(b). From here, we see that the thickness of Cb,D is at least cdN1/2(b) for some constant cd > 0. □\n\nb\n\nThe thickness estimate holds for all Cb,D,j because of the self-similarity.\n\nHowever, we cannot directly use Theorem 11 to obtain intersections between different missing digit sets without checking the conditions (2,3).\n\nConsider different integers b1,...,bk ∈ Z[i] with large enough norms so that Theorem 11 applies. Consider the logarithmic map\n\nlog : a ∈ Z[i] → (log |a|,arg a) ∈ (0,∞) × T. The first component is the standard log for positive numbers. The second component is the argument of a as a complex number.3 For simplicity, let us first look at the two integers b1,b2. We claim that there are infinitely many positive rational integer pairs (l1,l2) such that bl\n\n# 1 /bl\n\n# 2 is close to one. In fact, consider the set P = {bl\n\n1\n\n2\n\n# 1 /bl\n\n2 : (l1,l2) ∈ Z2>0} ⊂ C.\n\n1\n\n2\n\n3In general, such a logarithmic map can be defined in each Galois embedding individually.\n\n",
